call_details.response:
The current board configuration has pieces only in the bottom two rows, with Black (o) occupying (5,0), (5,4) and (4,3) while White (x) occupies the consecutive cells (5,1)-(5,3). No column is full, so every column 0-6 is legal.

A direct four-in-a-row is impossible for White on this turn: the sole existing three-in-a-row is already blocked at both ends by opposing stones, and none of the seven possible landing squares is collinear with two of White’s stones. Consequently Black likewise possesses no immediate winning reply. Tactical forcing moves (immediate threats or double-threat forks) are therefore absent, and the decision reduces to maximising long-term winning potential.

Each candidate landing square was scored by the number of still-open four-in-a-row groups that contain it:

- (3,3) lies on 11 open groups (4 horizontal, 1 vertical, 3 of each diagonal direction);
- (4,2) lies on 9 groups;
- (4,4) lies on 8 groups;
- all remaining landings lie on 6 or fewer groups.

The square (3,3) is therefore the single most valuable empty cell on the board. Occupying it also permanently denies Black every vertical four-in-a-row in the centre column. Although playing (4,2) yields a high score, it simultaneously lowers the next empty cell of column 2 to (3,2), allowing Black to obtain a free three-in-a-row on the following move; that concession outweighs the modest difference in raw group count.

Hence the unique optimal placement is the highest-value central square.

Final Answer: 3

